---
/**
 * Medicare Advantage (Part C).
 *
 * Order matters here: what it is, then how it differs from the alternative
 * (the compare component), then how to choose one — and only then the enroll
 * button. Someone who reaches "Simply Enroll" has been told to check the
 * network, the doctor, and the drug list first, which is the whole point.
 */
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import CompareMaMedigap from '../../components/CompareMaMedigap.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
import { links, isPending, externalAttrs } from '../../data/links';

const title = 'Medicare Advantage (Part C) Explained | Lisa’s Insurance Agency';
const description =
  'How Medicare Advantage plans work, the difference between HMO, PPO and PFFS, how they compare to a Medicare Supplement, and the three things to check before you enroll.';

const enrollPending = isPending(links.sunfireMedicareAdvantage);

const types = [
  {
    tag: 'HMO',
    name: 'Health Maintenance Organization',
    copy: 'Care stays within the plan’s network, and you generally choose a primary care doctor who coordinates your care and refers you to specialists. Outside the network, coverage is usually limited to emergencies. In exchange, HMO plans often carry the lowest premiums of the three.',
  },
  {
    tag: 'PPO',
    name: 'Preferred Provider Organization',
    copy: 'You can go outside the network and still be covered, though it costs you more than staying in. Referrals generally aren’t required, so you can go straight to a specialist. More freedom than an HMO, usually at a higher premium.',
  },
  {
    tag: 'PFFS',
    name: 'Private Fee-for-Service',
    copy: 'The plan sets what it pays providers and what you pay. Any provider who accepts Medicare may treat you — but they can decide appointment by appointment whether to accept the plan’s terms, so it’s worth confirming before each visit. Less common than the other two.',
  },
];

const checks = [
  {
    title: 'Check the network',
    /* "Every plan has one" was inaccurate: a PFFS plan may have no network at
       all, which the plan-types list on this same page already says. Corrected
       for CMS accuracy — see the compliance note in the "What it is" section. */
    copy: 'Most plans have one, and it’s local — a PFFS plan may not, in which case what matters is whether your providers will accept the plan’s terms. A plan that’s a good fit in the next county over may not be here. Start by seeing which carriers even operate where you live.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Confirm your providers are in it',
    copy: 'Look up each doctor you actually see — primary care, every specialist, and the hospital you’d want to use. Directories go out of date, so confirming with the office directly is worth the phone call.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Confirm your prescriptions are covered',
    copy: 'Each plan has its own drug list, and it decides both whether a medicine is covered and what tier it sits on. Have your actual prescriptions and doses in front of you — this is where plans that looked identical stop being identical.',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Do I still have Medicare if I join an Advantage plan?',
    a: 'Yes. You keep Medicare and keep paying your Part B premium; the plan becomes the way you receive your Part A and Part B benefits instead of receiving them directly from Medicare.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I have an Advantage plan and a Medigap policy?',
    a: 'No. They’re two different approaches to the same problem, and you choose one. A Medigap policy cannot be used to cover costs under a Medicare Advantage plan.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need a separate Part D plan?',
    a: 'Usually not — many Medicare Advantage plans include prescription coverage in the plan. Some don’t, though, so it’s something to check rather than assume. If your plan includes it, you generally can’t add a separate Part D plan on top.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if my doctor leaves the network?',
    a: 'Networks can change during the year. If it happens, call me — depending on the circumstances and the timing there may be options, and I’d rather hear about it early than at the next enrollment window.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When can I change plans?',
    a: 'The Annual Enrollment Period each autumn is the main window, and there’s a further window early in the year for people already in an Advantage plan. Certain life events — a move, a change in eligibility — can open a Special Enrollment Period as well.',
  },
];
---

<Page
  title={title}
  description={description}
  rawTitle
  eyebrow="Medicare · Part C"
  heading="Medicare Advantage, start to finish."
  intro="An all-in-one alternative to Original Medicare, run by a private carrier. Here’s how it works, how it differs from a Supplement, and the three checks to make before you sign anything."
>
  <!-- ─── What it is ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section" id="what-it-is" aria-labelledby="what-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="crumbs"><a href="/medicare">Medicare</a> / Medicare Advantage</p>
      <p class="eyebrow">What it is</p>

      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="what-heading">A single plan that provides all the benefits of Medicare Parts A and B</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          You’re receiving Medicare benefits through a private insurance company’s
          Medicare Advantage plan rather than directly through Original Medicare
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A Medicare Advantage (Part C) plan is another way to receive your
          Medicare Part A and Part B benefits through a Medicare-approved private
          insurance company. These plans must cover all medically necessary
          services covered by Original Medicare. Most plans also include
          prescription drug coverage (Part D) and may offer additional benefits,
          such as dental, vision, hearing, and fitness benefits. Benefits, provider
          networks, and out-of-pocket costs vary by plan
        </p>

        {/* COMPLIANCE DISCLOSURE — NOT client-supplied copy. MA-04's replacement
            states that Advantage plans must cover all medically necessary
            services covered by Original Medicare; hospice is the standing CMS
            exception, and the paragraph MA-04 replaced used to say so. Written
            to match how Medicare.gov states it. Needs Lisa's sign-off, but the
            page should not ship without the carve-out disclosed. */}
        <p>
          <strong>Hospice care is the exception.</strong> If you elect hospice,
          Original Medicare Part A covers that care even while you stay enrolled
          in your Medicare Advantage plan. The plan keeps covering services that
          aren’t related to your terminal condition, as long as you keep paying
          its premiums.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="chips">
        <span class="chip">
          <svg class="mark" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M9 16.2 4.8 12l-1.4 1.4L9 19 21 7l-1.4-1.4z"/>
          </svg>
          Yearly out-of-pocket maximum
        </span>
        <span class="chip">
          <svg class="mark" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M9 16.2 4.8 12l-1.4 1.4L9 19 21 7l-1.4-1.4z"/>
          </svg>
          Drug coverage often built in
        </span>
        <span class="chip">
          <svg class="mark" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M9 16.2 4.8 12l-1.4 1.4L9 19 21 7l-1.4-1.4z"/>
          </svg>
          You keep paying your Part B premium
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Plan types ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section section--navy" id="types" aria-labelledby="types-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Plan types</p>

      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="types-heading">HMO, PPO, PFFS.</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Same idea, three sets of rules about where you can go and whether you
          need a referral first.
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="def-list">
        {types.map((type) => (
          <div class="def-row">
            <dt>
              <span class="def-tag">{type.tag}</span>
              {type.name}
            </dt>
            <dd>{type.copy}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <p class="source-note">
        Which of these are sold where you live varies by county, and so does the
        list of carriers offering them. There are other, less common plan types as
        well — including plans built for people with specific conditions or who
        also have Medicaid. Ask me what’s available at your address.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Compare ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section" id="compare" aria-labelledby="compare-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Compare</p>

      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="compare-heading">
          Want to know how Medicare Advantage compares to Medicare Supplement?
        </h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Here they are side by side. Nothing below is about price — it’s about how
          the two are built, which is what actually decides the fit.
        </p>
      </div>

      <CompareMaMedigap stay="advantage" id="compare-heading" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── How to choose ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section section--navy" id="how-to-choose" aria-labelledby="choose-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">How to choose</p>

      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="choose-heading">Three things to check, in this order.</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Do these before you compare premiums. A cheaper plan that doesn’t cover
          your cardiologist isn’t cheaper.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="steps">
        {checks.map((check) => (
          <li>
            <h3>{check.title}</h3>
            <p>{check.copy}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      <div class="medicare-cta">
        <p>
          Once those three check out, enrolling is quick. You can do it yourself
          below, or call me and we’ll go through it together — the premium is
          identical either way.
        </p>

        <div class="actions actions--stack">
          {enrollPending ? (
            <>
              {/* TODO: links.sunfireMedicareAdvantage — Sunfire "Simply Enroll".
                  Until it's live this is the call path, never a dead button. */}
              <a class="btn btn--accent" href={site.phoneHref}>Simply Enroll</a>
              <span class="chip">Online enrollment link coming soon — for now, call or text</span>
            </>
          ) : (
            <a class="btn btn--accent" href={links.sunfireMedicareAdvantage} {...externalAttrs}>
              Simply Enroll
            </a>
          )}
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/contact">Questions? Contact Lisa</a>
        </div>
        <p class="microline">Calling connects you with Lisa, a licensed insurance agent.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── FAQ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Questions</p>

      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="faq-heading">Questions about Advantage plans.</h2>
      </div>

      <Faq items={faqs} schema />

      <div class="callout" style="margin-top: clamp(40px, 5vw, 64px)">
        <h3>If your plan doesn’t include drug coverage</h3>
        <p>
          You’ll want to understand Part D before you decide — including the
          late-enrollment penalty, which is permanent once it applies.
          <a href="/medicare/prescription-drugs">Read about prescription drug coverage</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    heading="Let’s check your doctors before you choose."
    copy="Have your prescriptions and your doctors’ names handy. Fifteen minutes on the phone will tell you more than an afternoon of comparing plans online."
  />
</Page>
